<archimate:ArchimateDiagramModel
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xmlns:archimate="http://www.archimatetool.com/archimate"
    name="SAF Business Architecture"
    id="id-c12e67400c814f4680d10de4ba25634a"
    documentation="This diagram describes a generic business architecture for statistical production that transforms data from external sources into published statistics through a series of well-defined functions and quality states. The architecture decomposes the statistical production process into five domain business functions (Data Acquisition, Standardization, Data Editing, Data Processing, and Dissemination) and four supporting functions (Process Design, Process Implementation, Process Execution, and Process Management), complemented by Metadata Management and Data Management capabilities.The key principle of this architecture is its focus on clearly defined data states (from State #0 Source Data through State #6 Consumed Data) that act as interfaces between functions. Each state represents data at a specific quality level with explicit requirements, enabling different statistical domains to share and reuse data at standardized quality milestones. This state-based approach promotes interoperability by establishing common data formats and classifications, enhances transparency through comprehensive data lineage and quality documentation at each stage, supports reproducibility by clearly defining the transformations and processes applied to move between states, and enables innovation by allowing teams to independently develop and improve their processes while adhering to standardized input and output specifications.By separating concerns between domain functions (what is done) and supporting functions (how it is designed, implemented, executed, and managed), the architecture provides flexibility for statistical organizations to modernize specific capabilities without disrupting the entire production chain, fostering continuous improvement and adaptation to new methodologies and technologies.">
